fix(pip): Treat any optic as screen locked while aiming, not only magnifying ones

The ring inside the thermal sight only appears while the view is moving fast and settles when it
stops, which is the signature of temporal ghosting rather than of anything drawn in the sight.

The engine already had the right idea in StableOverlaysActive(): a screen locked overlay - the sight's
lens or reticle, drawn in the weapon layer - does not follow the scene motion vectors, so while one
is up the upscaler's history is invalidated every frame and the upscale stays spatial. The last
clause of that predicate gated on hud_fov_params.x > 1.25, i.e. on the optic's magnification factor,
and that number is not usable: the pack's optics report factors at or below 1. The log line for an
M82 carrying a sight reads rtZoom=0.0, so a screen type thermal sight was excluded from the rule,
its image was reprojected with the scene motion vectors, and the result trails behind the camera -
a ring that only exists in movement, exactly as reported.

The predicate now uses the engine's own aiming facts instead: the ADS progress from the game, plus
"this weapon has an optic with lens data" (the optic facts the game publishes for the second
viewport, or a magnifying optic). That covers screen type sights the wearer's magnification factor
says nothing about, and still excludes plain iron sight aiming.

r__svp_lcd_flat from the previous commit stays in place, off by default: it was based on the
reflection hypothesis, which this report disproved, and it is inert rather than wrong, so it is left
for the pack author's benefit rather than removed mid stream.